BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Jacobs School of Music
DTSTART:20261118T213000Z
DTEND:20261118T230000Z
LOCATION:WOODBURN HALL
GEO:39.168107;-86.5201
SUMMARY:Migrant Remixing: Drag and the Transatlantic Circulation of Andea
 n Latinx Aesthetics
DESCRIPTION:As a migrant in the United States\, Peruvian soprano Yma Suma
 c mesmerized mid-twentieth century audiences with her vocal range and her
  Inca-inspired performance attire. Taking Sumac as a point of departure\,
  this presentation examines the work of contemporary drag performers who 
 similarly draw on Andean cultural imagery as migrant artists in Spain. Va
 squez Toral traces a transatlantic circulation of Andean visual culture a
 nd Indigeneity through a performance lens that I term "migrant remixing."
  He focuses on the work of drag performers Gad Yola (Peru) and Inti (Boli
 via)\, examining how their music and drag art are infused with decolonial
  and anti-racist political urgencies. Collectively\, they engage Spain's 
 colonial legacies and the challenges Latinx migrants face in contemporary
  Spanish society by centering queer Indigenous histories and LGBTQ cultur
 es from the Andes.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  As a migrant in the United States\, Per
 uvian soprano Yma Sumac mesmerized mid-twentieth century audiences with h
 er vocal range and her Inca-inspired performance attire. Taking Sumac as 
 a point of departure\, this presentation examines the work of contemporar
 y drag performers who similarly draw on Andean cultural imagery as migran
 t artists in Spain. Vasquez Toral traces a transatlantic circulation of A
 ndean visual culture and Indigeneity through a performance lens that I te
 rm "migrant remixing." He focuses on the work of drag performers Gad Yola
  (Peru) and Inti (Bolivia)\, examining how their music and drag art are i
 nfused with decolonial and anti-racist political urgencies. Collectively\
 , they engage Spain's colonial legacies and the challenges Latinx migrant
 s face in contemporary Spanish society by centering queer Indigenous hist
 ories and LGBTQ cultures from the Andes.</p>
UID:20261118T213000Z-2475690@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20261106T092124Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2474508-migrant-remixing-drag-and-t
 he-transatlantic
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferenc
 es,Performances, shows, and films
LAST-MODIFIED:20261106T142124Z
